  - q: "What is the best month to visit the Dubai desert?"
    a: "November to March. Daytime highs sit around 24 to 30 degrees, evenings are cool enough for a jacket, and any slot in the day works. October and April are still comfortable at the edges of the day. If you are choosing purely on weather and have a free hand, January and February are the easiest months to plan around."
  - q: "Is it too hot for the desert in summer?"
    a: "Not at the right hour. From June to September the middle of the day is genuinely unpleasant in the open desert, so sessions move to shortly after sunrise or to late afternoon once the sand has cooled. At those hours it is warm rather than punishing, and the dunes are emptier than in winter."
  - q: "What time of day is best for a desert ride?"
    a: "Late afternoon for most people, because you ride as the light turns and the red dunes photograph at their best in the hour before sunset. Sunrise is the other answer, and it is the better one for photographs of untouched sand, for anyone travelling with children, and for the whole summer."
  - q: "Do the red dunes really look red?"
    a: "In low light, yes. The sand at Lahbab carries enough iron oxide to read orange to deep red in the hour after sunrise and the hour before sunset. At midday the same sand looks beige in a photograph. The time of day changes the colour far more than the season does."
  - q: "Does wind stop desert sessions?"
    a: "More often than heat. Blowing sand cuts visibility in a dune field and makes a guided line unsafe, and it can arrive on a day the forecast calls clear. If conditions make the route unsafe we contact you before you travel and move the booking at no cost, or refund it in full if no other date works."
  - q: "Is it worth going in Ramadan or over public holidays?"
    a: "Yes, and timings shift rather than stop. UAE public holidays and the December to February peak are the busiest weeks in the dune field, so book earlier and expect more vehicles around. Outside those, the desert is quieter than most visitors assume."
  - q: "Does rain affect the desert in Dubai?"
    a: "Rain is rare and brief, and wet sand actually rides well because it packs firmer. What follows rain matters more: the days afterwards can bring stronger wind. If a booking is affected we move it rather than run it."
  - q: "When are the dunes least crowded?"
    a: "Early morning in any month, and the whole of the summer. If having the dune field mostly to yourself matters more than comfortable temperatures, a June sunrise session is as empty as it gets here."
