## The season, in one table

| Period | Daytime | What to book |
|---|---|---|
| November to March | 24 to 30 degrees | Any slot. Late afternoon is the pick |
| October and April | Warm at midday | Morning or late afternoon |
| May to September | Hot | Sunrise, or late afternoon once the sand cools |

That is the whole seasonal answer and most guides stop there. The more useful half is below.

## The hour matters more than the month

A midday session in February is warmer than a sunrise session in July. That sentence surprises people and it is the reason we push slots rather than seasons.

Two hours in the day do the work. **The hour after sunrise** gives you cool sand, an empty dune field and the cleanest photographs, because nobody has driven lines across it yet. **The hour before sunset** gives you the colour, the sunset stop, and an evening that carries on into a [camp dinner](/desert-safari-dubai/) if you want it to.

Between those, in any month, the sand is the same sand and it photographs beige.

We run from 7am to 11pm, seven days a week, which exists specifically so the early slot is bookable.

## Summer is not the problem people think it is

June to September gets written off, and that is overcautious.

The middle of a July day in the open desert is genuinely unpleasant and we would talk you out of it. Shortly after sunrise in July is warm, quiet and completely workable, and the dune field is emptier than it ever is in January.

What summer really costs you is flexibility. You get two usable windows instead of a whole day, and if your schedule cannot accommodate a 6am start then winter is the better trip.

## Wind is the thing that actually cancels rides

Heat is predictable. Wind is not, and it is what moves bookings here.

Blowing sand cuts visibility across a dune field, which makes a guided line unsafe for a group. It arrives on days a forecast app calls clear, because what matters is sand in the air rather than the number in the app.

Our rule is simple and it is worth knowing before you book anything: **if wind, heat or visibility make the route unsafe, we contact you before you travel and move the booking at no cost.** If no other date works, you get a full refund. Nothing is paid at booking, so there is usually nothing to reclaim.

Anyone who tells you they run in all conditions is telling you something about their judgement rather than their vehicles.

## Booking around a short trip

If you are in Dubai for three days, book the desert on the first full one.

That is not a sales line. It is because a weather move needs somewhere to move to, and a booking on your last afternoon has nowhere to go. Guests who book early in the trip almost never lose the session; guests who leave it to the final evening sometimes do.

The other reason is that people who enjoy it come back within the same trip, and that only works if there are days left.

## What each month is actually like

**November to February** is the easy answer and the busiest window. Cool enough that midday works, cold enough after dark that the jacket everyone leaves behind gets missed.

**March and October** are the quiet value months. Warm at noon, perfect at either end of the day, and far fewer vehicles in the dune field than in the peak.

**April and May** tip over during the day but hold up beautifully at sunrise, and the light in April is the best of the year.

**June to September** is two windows rather than a day, and it rewards anyone willing to get up early with an empty desert.

## What the timing does not change

The price. A [buggy](/dune-buggy-dubai/) costs the same at sunrise as at four in the afternoon, and so does a [quad](/quad-bike-dubai/). There is no peak-hour surcharge and no seasonal rate.

So the only thing you are optimising when you pick a slot is how much you enjoy it, which is a rare position to be in when booking anything.

We have run our base at Al Awir since 2020 and taken more than 17,000 guests into the Lahbab dunes. The single best session of the year here is a morning in late November, and almost nobody books it.
